==================== dV_ELL_Earth2014_5480_plusGRS80 - a degree-5480 topographic gravity model ==========================

MODEL DESCRIPTION:
dV_ELL_Earth2014_5480 is a spherical harmonic model of the gravitational potential implied by Earth's topographic
masses (i.e. rock, water and ice masses) with respect to the GRS80 ellipsoid.  Earth's topography is modelled without
isostatic compensation. The model comprises the complete gravitational potential of four rigorously modelled
volumetric-layers of the Earth2014 (Hirt&Rexer,2015) topographic model suite:

   Layer       Density      Upper bound       Lower bound         Layer-Model          Data sources
               [kg/m^3]
Crust-layer  |   2670   |    BED2014      |      GRS80      |   dV_ELL_Crust2014  |    SRTM V4.1 & SRTM30_PLUS
Ocean-layer  |   1030   | SUR2014-ICE2014 |     BED2014     |   dV_ELL_Ocean2014  |    SRTM30_PLUS
Lake-layer*  |   1000   | SUR2014-ICE2014 |     BED2014     |   dV_ELL_Lakes2014  |    SRTM30_PLUS
Ice-layer**  |    917   |    SUR2014      | SUR2014-ICE2014 |   dV_ELL_Ice2014    |    BEDMAP2, GBT v3

* accounts for Earth's major lakes (Superior, Michigan, Huron, Erie, Ontario and Baikal) and the Caspian Sea.
** accounts for Earth's major ice sheets over Greenland and Antarctica.

For citation of the model please use Rexer et al (2017) or Rexer (2017). The model has been computed using the harmonic
combination method (HCM) (Claessens & Hirt, 2013) along with the layer correction approach (LCA) (Rexer et al., 2016).
Since the reference for the layer boundaries is the GRS80 ellipsoid, dV_ELL_Earth2014 is an ellipsoidal topographic
potential model. This model is equivalent to its degree-2190 variant in terms of methods and input data used for its
creation, except for the higher resolution (i.e. higher maximum spherical harmonic degree), whereby the convergence
of the binominal series expansions was carefully ensured.

Potential models representing the potential of the single layers (crust, ocean, lake or ice) are also
available:

http://ddfe.curtin.edu.au/gravitymodels/Earth2014/potential_model/

A full account of the model is given in Rexer et al (2017) and Rexer (2017), which should also be used as reference
 when using the model.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

REFERENCES:

Rexer M., Hirt C. and  Pail R. (2017) High-resolution global forward modelling - A degree-5480 global ellipsoidal
topographic potential model, EGU2017-7725,  European Geosciences Union General Assembly 2017, Vienna, 25/04/2017.

Rexer, M. (2017) Spectral Solutions to the Topographic Potential in the context of High-Resolution Global Gravity
Field Modelling, Dissertation, Technische Universitaet Muenchen, http://mediatum.ub.tum.de?id=1349781.

Rexer, M., Hirt, C., Claessens, S., Tenzer, R. (2016) "Layer-based modelling of the Earth's gravitational potential
up to 10km-scale in spherical harmonics in spherical and ellipsoidal approximation", Surveys in Geophysics,
doi:10.1007/s10712-016-9382-2.

Hirt, C. and Rexer, M. (2015) "Earth2014: 1' shape, topography, bedrock and ice-sheet models - Available as gridded
data and degree 10,800 spherical harmonics", International Journal of Applied Earth Observation and Geoinformation,
Vol. 39, pp 103-112, Elsevier, DOI: 10.1016/j.jag.2015.03.001.

Claessens, S., Hirt, C. (2013) Ellipsoidal topographic potential - new solutions for spectral forward gravity
Modelling of topography with respect to a reference ellipsoid; Journal of Geophysical Research (JGR) Solid Earth,
Vol. 118, DOI: 10.1002/2013JB010457.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORTANT REMARKS:
1) In spherical harmonic syntheses (SHS) this model always should be evaluated to its maximum degree (Nmax=5480),
since the last 80 coefficients account for the ellipsoidal shape of Earth. Neglecting this "tail" of coefficients
or truncating the model at lower degrees during SHS will result in severe error patterns, especially at high
latitudes.
2) In spherical harmonic syntheses, the reference surface should always be the GRS80 ellipsoid or be defined
through topographic heights above the GRS80 ellipsoid.
3) In this version of dV_ELL_Earth_5480 the normal potential (GRS80) is not included, thus the coefficients describe
the disturbing potential generated by the topographic masses. The version dV_ELL_Earth2014_5480_plusGRS80 is also
available and additionally contains the following zonal (m=0) coeffiecients

        C 0,0 =   1.00000000000000e-00
        C 2,0 =  -4.84166854896119e-04
        C 4,0 =   7.90304072883172e-07
        C 6,0 =  -1.68725117564870e-09
        C 8,0 =   3.46053239783084e-12
        C10,0 =  -2.65006217677409e-15
        C12,0 =  -4.10788001637464e-17
        C14,0 =   4.47176179093085e-19
        C16,0 =  -3.46361902667213e-21

which may likewise be added to this version in order to obtain a version that is e.g. compatible with synthesis
software that - by default - subtracts the normal potential.

